     1	# V1 release-key custody and release authorization (DRAFT for the author's correction)
     2	
     3	Status: DRAFT, owner instance, 2026-10-17. This procedure establishes the gate in proposed A10.4.2: the release key
     4	must be independent of the DNS credential, the repository key and the AWS account. The author corrects it. A
     5	non-author review checks it before the 30 Oct freeze. It contains no secret and must never contain one.
     6	
     7	## 1. What the release key does
     8	
     9	The release key signs each verifier release and the trust file in it. The trust file lists the DNS root keys, the
    10	repository keys, the Bitcoin header pins and (if A10 option (e) is adopted) the service keys for each window. A
    11	person who has the release key can make a verifier trust anything. So the release key is the most important key in
    12	V1.
    13	
    14	## 2. Separation rules
    15	
    16	These rules separate access paths, not people. The author controls every credential, and V1 claims no separation of
    17	control. The requirement is this: a person who captures the DNS credential, the repository key or the AWS account
    18	does not get a path to the release key, or a way to make the author sign a release.
    19	
    20	1. The release key is a different key from the repository key and from both service keys.
    21	2. The release key never exists on the operational machine. The operational machine is the NUC that holds the AWS
    22	   login, the Google Cloud login and the Tessera repository.
    23	3. The release key never exists on a machine that is connected to a network.
    24	4. No credential for DNS, AWS, Google Cloud or the repository is on the signing machine.
    25	5. Only the author can authorize a release. The owner instance prepares releases. The owner instance never holds
    26	   the release key or its passphrase.
    27	
    28	## 3. Custody
    29	
    30	- **Primary:** a YubiKey 5 with the OpenPGP applet. PIN and touch are required for each signature. If the YubiKeys
    31	  are not available before the freeze, the primary is an encrypted key file on the signing machine's removable
    32	  medium (the offline fallback). The custody record states which one is in use.
    33	- **Signing machine:** a computer that has no network connection when it signs. It can be a laptop with networking
    34	  disabled in hardware, or a live USB system that starts without network drivers. *(Author: say which machine.)*
    35	- **Backup:** two encrypted copies of the release key, made at the key ceremony, each on a separate USB drive. The
    36	  two drives are in two different physical places, and neither is with the NUC. The passphrase is on paper, not in
    37	  any computer or password manager that the NUC can reach. *(Author: confirm two places exist. Do not tell me where.)*
    38	
    39	## 4. Key ceremony (once)
    40	
    41	1. Start the signing machine with no network.
    42	2. Generate the release key (P-256, OpenPGP). Write down its fingerprint on paper.
    43	3. Make the two encrypted backups. Restore one backup on the signing machine and make a test signature with it, to
    44	   show that the backup works.
    45	4. If a YubiKey is the primary, load the key into it and set its PIN and touch policy.
    46	5. Put the public key on a removable medium. Copy it to the NUC.
    47	6. On the NUC, publish the public key and its fingerprint in the repository. Also publish them on wamason.com, so
    48	   that two channels show them.
    49	7. Write a dated custody record: the fingerprint, the custody type (YubiKey or offline file), the signing machine
    50	   type, and confirmation that the backups are in two places. Do not record where they are.
    51	
    52	Rehearse the whole ceremony once with a throwaway key before the real ceremony.
    53	
    54	## 5. Each release
    55	
    56	1. The owner instance prepares the release on the NUC: the verifier code and its trust file. It writes a one-page
    57	   **release summary**: what changed in the trust file since the last release (added entries only, because the trust
    58	   file is append-only), and the SHA-256 of the release archive.
    59	2. The owner instance copies the archive and the summary to a removable medium.
    60	3. The author reads the summary. The author checks that each added entry is expected. If an entry is not expected,
    61	   the author does not sign.
    62	4. On the signing machine, the author computes the SHA-256 of the archive again and compares it with the summary.
    63	   Then the author signs it.
    64	5. The author copies the signature back to the NUC. The owner instance publishes the release, its signature and
    65	   the summary, and makes an OpenTimestamps stamp of the release.
    66	
    67	The author's time for each release is about 15 minutes. V1 has one window, so it needs one release for the freeze
    68	and one for each later window.
    69	
    70	## 6. If something goes wrong
    71	
    72	- **Lost YubiKey:** restore from a backup at a new ceremony with a new YubiKey. The key does not change.
    73	- **The release key is suspected to be copied:** stop releases. Do not shorten or change an existing trust file.
    74	  The registered boundary applies: a release-key compromise is a distribution compromise. The author decides what
    75	  to publish.
    76	- **The NUC is compromised:** the release key is not on it, so the release key is safe. Recheck the next release
    77	  summary with special care, because the NUC prepared it.
    78	
    79	## 7. What this procedure does not do
    80	
    81	It does not protect a person who uses a false verifier release that was sent only to them. It does not make a
    82	capture of the DNS account or the repository key public. Those limits are in A10.
     1	# Tessera plain-language style (inspired by ASD-STE100)
     2	
     3	Owner instance, 2026-10-16. The author asked for documentation "written so someone whose native language isn't
     4	Claude can understand it". These rules adapt the main writing rules of ASD-STE100 (Simplified Technical English).
     5	We do not claim STE conformance: we have no approved STE dictionary check. We use the structure rules and our own
     6	dictionary.
     7	
     8	Revised 2026-10-16 after the faithfulness review of the A9 pilot (`docs/reviews/plain-pilot/CODEX-REVIEW.md`,
     9	"Proposed dictionary work"). The revision adds the entries that the review lists and repairs receipt, anchor time,
    10	window, authority record, key, AWS key, VALID and INVALID. It replaces "Google key" with "second key". It removes
    11	"receipt ID", because its object was not defined. These entries are owner proposals. A reviewer must check each
    12	new entry against its registered source before publication.
    13	
    14	## Rules
    15	
    16	1. One sentence gives one idea.
    17	2. A sentence has a maximum of 20 words if it tells the reader to do something, and 25 words if it describes something.
    18	3. A paragraph has a maximum of 6 sentences and one topic. A fragment such as a list item counts as a sentence.
    19	4. Use the active voice. Say who or what does the action.
    20	5. Use only the words in the dictionary below for project ideas. Each word has one meaning. Do not use a synonym.
    21	6. Use technical names (for example "DNSSEC", "Bitcoin", "SHA-256") without change. Explain each one when you first use it.
    22	7. Do not use idioms, metaphors or humour.
    23	8. Write steps and conditions as numbered or bulleted lists.
    24	9. Write "must" for a rule and "can" for a possibility. Do not use "should", "may" or "shall".
    25	10. Do not refer to a document section number in the body. Put references at the end.
    26	
    27	## Faithfulness
    28	
