   183	## A10.1 "Existed no later than T" is chain time, not UTC — OWNER DECISION, NARROW-CLAIM (review A01)
   184	
   185	A9 says the bytes and signatures "existed no later than a time T fixed by Bitcoin" (A9:36-37; again at :114,
   186	:133 and in C5, :144). A9 also says that header times are chain-time proxies (C5) and that a finite UTC cutoff
   187	"requires an additional, explicit backward-skew assumption that V1 does not establish" (:444-446). The ordinary
   188	reading of "no later than T" is a real-time bound, which A9 does not support.
   189	
   190	**Reading adopted.** "No later than T" means chain order: the bytes and exact signatures existed before the
   191	selected Bitcoin block was mined, because that block commits to their SHA-256 hash (under the registered
   192	cryptographic and chain assumptions). T is that block's header time, a chain-time value. It is not an upper bound
   193	in UTC: A2.1 registers no finite bound on B − C, and neither ε nor S is one. A UTC reading needs the additional
   194	backward-skew assumption that V1 does not establish. A10.1.1 recommends one such assumption, for a separate
   195	assessment only; this section does not depend on it.
   196	
   197	**Other registered uses of the same bound.** The scenario mapping (:177, :186), the issue order (:294-295) and
   198	guarantee 1 (:433-439) use T as the same clock bound. Each is qualified the same way (rows 5–9 of A10.7). These
   199	qualifications change the explanation of the safe decisions, not their verdicts, fixtures or case membership.
   200	Scenario i's parties' signature-checking duty and guarantee 1's `historical_security: NOT_EVALUATED` stay. The
   201	issue-order case is concrete: a signature made after a backward-skewed header's nominal UTC time, but before the
   202	block is mined, can fit the block's anchor.
   203	
   204	**Assembled C5 claim cell** (rows 4 and 17 of A10.7 both point here):
   205	
   206	> The frames **and their exact signatures** existed before the selected Bitcoin block was mined. T is that block's
   207	> header time, not a UTC upper bound. The selected block belongs to the qualifying Bitcoin attestation at the lowest
   208	> block height in the presented OTS proof, under A10.5's stages; T and its confirmations come from that same
   209	> attestation. The three-conjunct predicate holds at k = 6. Header times need not be monotone; selection uses chain
   210	> order, not the least header time (OWNER DECISION 2026-10-14, F03).
   211	
   212	C5's non-claims stay, with the reorganization residual amended by A10.3 (row 14). If the author adopts A10.1.1,
   213	row 21 adds its conditional sentence after this cell; the cell itself does not change.
   214	
   215	**A1.6.** A1.6 (:485-505) contains older, compressed clock and depth language. A1 stays standing. This amendment is
   216	a V1 qualification of that inherited clock wording; the unchanged historical prose does not itself establish a UTC
   217	bound. The changed sentences are rows 1–9 of A10.7.
   218	
   219	### A10.1.1 A conditional UTC assessment — RECOMMENDED (owner and Codex), BROADEN, AWAITING AUTHOR RULING
   220	
   221	Source: the decision memo, Part 1 (U1) and Part 3; its Codex review, F03, F04 and "Questions checked" 1; the
   222	tolerance evidence file. A10.1 stays as decided whatever the author rules here.
   223	
   224	**Notation.** h* is the receipt proof's selected inclusion height (A10.5). A2.1 counts it as confirmation one, so
   225	h*+5 is confirmation six. r(j) is the real (UTC) time at which block j was first produced.
   226	
   227	**Assumption A-UTC** (`assumption_id: "A-UTC-7200-6"`). *At least one of the six blocks h* … h*+5 has a header
   228	time no more than 7200 s before it was actually mined*, that is, `nTime(j) ≥ r(j) − 7200 s` for some j in
   229	[h*, h*+5]. A-UTC is an assumption of the UTC assessment only. It is not a Bitcoin consensus rule, the verifier
   230	does not check it, and no bundle carries evidence of it. It is a sufficient one-sided assumption, not a minimal one.
   231	Δ = 7200 s and m = 6 are frozen in the governing specification's catalog, not in the mutable trust file, and are
   232	not shown to be optimal.
   233	
   234	**Recommended claim text**, added after the A10.1 claim it qualifies (rows 20–21):
   235	
   236	> Under assumption A-UTC (at least one of the blocks h* … h*+5 has a header time no more than 7200 s before it was
   237	> actually mined; an assumption of the UTC assessment, not a consensus rule, and not checked by the verifier), the
   238	> bytes and both signatures existed before U = max nTime(h* … h*+5) + 7200 s, in UTC. V1 reports U only in
   239	> `utc_assessment`, beside its assumption identifier, outside the six stable fields. A2.1's refusal to bound the
   240	> observation lag B − C is unchanged.
   241	
   242	**Derivation** (memo, checked by Codex). (1) `A_sig` existed before r(h*), because h*'s merkle root commits to
   243	`SHA256(A_sig)` under the registered commitment and history assumptions. (2) r(h*) ≤ r(j) for every j ≥ h*,
   244	because block j commits to a chain that contains h*. (3) A-UTC supplies j in [h*, h*+5] with r(j) ≤ nTime(j) + 7200.
   245	(4) So r(h*) ≤ r(j) ≤ nTime(j) + 7200 ≤ U. The chain-order claims of A10.1 rest on steps 1–2 only.
   246	
   247	**Output contract** (memo, F03). `utc_assessment` contains `assumption_id: "A-UTC-7200-6"` and `utc_upper_bound`
   248	(RFC 3339 string or null). It is computed from the receipt proof's selected h* and its six qualified headers. Its
   249	bound is conditional on A-UTC and the existing commitment/history assumptions. It is null exactly when T is null.
   250	It does not change the six-field V1 stable result (A9:162, A9.4). Reporting a value with INVALID or UNVERIFIABLE
   251	does not establish a valid service receipt or valid service signatures. U is never a stable field, and V1's
   252	documentation and relying-party text never state U without A-UTC beside it.
   253	
   254	**Arithmetic and the 2106 boundary** (memo, F04). Verification of a preserved V1 receipt does not consult the
   255	current clock. Each bundled nTime is the profile's unsigned epoch-seconds value, widened before arithmetic:
   256	`U_seconds = 7200 + max(uint64(nTime(h*)), …, uint64(nTime(h*+5)))`, encoded as an RFC 3339 UTC string without
   257	reduction modulo 2^32 (for max nTime = 4294967295, U = `2106-02-07T08:28:15Z`, not a 1970 time). This 64-bit
   258	arithmetic covers preserved headers. Timestamp representation for Bitcoin blocks produced after the 2106 rollover
   259	of the 32-bit field is a Bitcoin protocol matter outside V1; the profile's serial-time unwrapping applies to DNSSEC
   260	RRSIG fields only.
   261	
   262	**What the signature does and does not do.** If the author adopts this section, his signature adopts A-UTC as a
   263	trust assumption. It does not establish that miners satisfy it, and nothing in V1 can.
   264	
   265	**Justification of the tolerance against the intended adversary**
   266	(`docs/decisions/2026-10-17-a-utc-tolerance-evidence.md`). The intended adversary is A9's backdater (A9:40): bytes
   267	that exist only at real time e, presented with a receipt whose U is earlier than e.
   268	- **Failure condition.** A-UTC fails, and U can be wrong, only if the miners of all six confirming blocks set
   269	  lagging timestamps: every block h* … h*+5 has a header time more than 7200 s before it was mined.
   270	- **A non-mining backdater** can choose only when to stamp and which proof to present. Lowest-height selection fixes
   271	  the result within a presented proof, but does not eliminate choice among available presentations. The UTC
   272	  assessment relies on A-UTC for the selected segment in every case. Over 559,718 retained complete six-block
   273	  windows (heights 369,715–941,944, 2015–2026; Bitcoin Block Arrival Time Dataset, multiple observers, CC0), every
   274	  window contained a block whose recorded arrival lag was at most 1,065 s. The minimum computed U minus the
   275	  inclusion block's recorded first-seen time was 6,135 s (median 9,908 s); U's UTC interpretation remains
   276	  conditional on A-UTC. If the selected observer readings do not understate production time, these observations
   277	  support A-UTC for the sampled windows. Observer clock error is not bounded here. The dataset's ±7200 s quality
   278	  filter and excluded incomplete windows prevent an inference about the frequency of A-UTC failures outside the
   279	  retained sample. The largest single-block lag observed was 3,918 s; the median was 18 s. A mempool.space sample of
   280	  481 blocks (2024) and Bowden et al. (arXiv:1801.07447, 2018) are consistent with this.
   281	- **Consensus.** Consensus requires each timestamp to exceed the median of the preceding eleven header times. The
   282	  evidence reproduces 545 windows in which every recorded first-seen-minus-MTP value exceeds 7200 s. These MTPs use
   283	  the actual predecessor timestamps and the observer-clock qualification. The count does not bound a coalition
   284	  changing the six timestamps, since changes within the window also change later MTPs. A majority coalition can
   285	  sustain lagging timestamps; A-UTC is not consensus protection against that adversary.
   286	- **Limits of the evidence.** The dataset's own quality check rejects any arrival more than 7200 s from its header
   287	  time, so it cannot show a lag above the threshold; its distribution below the threshold has no observation
   288	  between 3,918 s and 7,200 s. Observer clock error is not separated. The data describe ordinary miner behaviour,
   289	  not a deliberate coalition.
   290	- Δ equals Bitcoin's node-local forward tolerance; that match does not validate it. m = 6 reuses the k = 6 headers

