   322	## A10.3 Reorganization depth, at k = 6 and `DepthK = k − 1` — OWNER DECISION, NARROW-CLAIM (review A03)
   323	
   324	A9 excludes "Bitcoin history deeper than k" (:49-50) and calls "Reorganization deeper than k" a residual (C5,
   325	:144). A9 also imports k = 6 under `DepthK = k − 1` (:105) and A2.1's count, in which the including block is the
   326	first confirmation.
   327	
   328	**Definition.** The depth of a reorganization is the number of blocks it removes from the tip of the chain. A
   329	reorganization of depth d removes every block that has d or fewer confirmations when it occurs. At k = 6
   330	confirmations, five blocks follow the anchor block; P5c's `depth` counts those five (`depth = c − 1`, so
   331	`DepthK = 5`).
   332	
   333	**Correction.** The registered assumption is that anchors with k or more confirmations are permanent (A1.6:
   334	"anchors at depth ≥ k are treated as permanent"). P5c's `Reorg` action can orphan an anchor only while
   335	`depth < DepthK`, that is, at five or fewer confirmations. So a reorganization of depth exactly k, which removes an
   336	anchor with k confirmations, is outside both. A9's "deeper than k" read literally leaves that case inside the
   337	claim, with no evidence. The excluded residual is therefore **a reorganization of depth k or more**. This narrows
   338	A9's literal prose claim at equality. It changes neither the model nor its permanence assumption, and withdraws no
   339	checked model result. This does not affect C4's chain-order gap (`g + 5 < r`), which the review found correct. The
   340	changed sentences are rows 13–14 of A10.7.
