   342	## A10.4 Authority capture: honest signed material protected; publication remains an issuer duty; key substitution — RECOMMENDED (owner; option (e) also Codex), NARROW-CLAIM and, under option (e), BROADEN, AWAITING AUTHOR RULING (review A04)
   343	
   344	A9's adversary sentence includes "anyone who captures authority-channel credentials while signing capability stays
   345	uncompromised (A8.3's sense of capture)" (:41-42). It names no set of receipts and no object for "signing
   346	capability". A8.3 (:72-76) bounds the tested property: under capture, "a captor cannot reattribute honest frames,
   347	alter honest signer sets, or carry honest signatures onto other bytes or keys". That property concerns honest
   348	service material, not only receipts issued before disclosure; the capture fixtures disclose the credentials before
   349	the honest signing processes run. A8.5 (:123-124) puts "preventing a captured or merged authority from issuing new
   350	attestations" out of scope. A captor holding both required channel credentials can sign and anchor a key assertion
   351	that names its own keys, without publishing it; receipts under those keys can then pass V1. Capture of only one
   352	required channel does not generally suffice.
   353	
   354	**The author's standard** (2026-10-08, `docs/guarantee-tiers-DRAFT.md`, "Authority capture"): *"perhaps the right
   355	way to attack the problem is distribution and admission that governance changes are out of scope"*, and
   356	*"Authority capture must be visible in such instances. For an adjudicator to be convinced incorrectly requires the
   357	takeover be invisible."* The owner summarizes it as: prevent, no; rewrite the past, no; visible, yes. The summary
   358	is the owner's, not a quotation. These words are the motivation for A10.4. They are not evidence that the visibility
   359	property is established. No option below establishes it: option (e) prevents acceptance by a scoped set of relying
   360	parties; it does not ensure that anyone detects or publicly exposes a capture.
   361	
   362	**Recommended text.** Replace the adversary clause with:
   363	
   364	> anyone who captures authority-channel credentials (the DNS credential or the repository key, including both
   365	> together) while the original service keys stay secret and their signing capability stays uncompromised (A8.3's
   366	> sense of capture), as to frames honestly signed by those service keys, whether before or after credential
   367	> disclosure: such a captor cannot reattribute those frames to other keys or another service, alter their honest
   368	> signer sets, or carry their signatures onto other bytes or keys (A8.3);
   369	
   370	and add to "The boundaries", before its last sentence:
   371	
   372	> V1 does not prevent a captor of both required authority-channel credentials from issuing new receipts under a key
   373	> assertion that names the captor's own keys, even while the original service keys remain uncompromised. Public
   374	> publication of the key-assertion repository remains an issuer duty (A9.7, "Changed in effect"). If the
   375	> substituted assertion is publicly published, a third party can inspect the substituted keys. The verifier does
   376	> not check publication; V1 does not guarantee that a captor publishes the assertion, that capture is detected, or
   377	> that new receipts are publicly visible.
   378	
   379	Under option (e), row 26 replaces this boundary's first sentence with a scoped one (A10.4.1).
   380	
   381	Backdating stays with the temporal checks (C4's chain-order pre-commitment, C5) under their own assumptions; A8.3's
   382	runs do not test it (A8.3). Whole-author-sphere capture stays excluded (R-2.15). Guarantee 2's proviso
   383	"provided the authority channels are not also captured" (:443) is unchanged and consistent with this text.
   384	
   385	**Options for the author.** (a) Adopt the bounded honest-material claim above, with the new-receipt limitation and
   386	conditional publication statement. (b) Deliberately restrict even that claim to receipts honestly issued before
   387	capture; this gives up the honest post-capture material covered by A8.3's stated property and must be recorded as
   388	an additional author choice. (c) Extend presentation protection to arbitrary new receipts under captor-selected
   389	keys for every relying party, with issuer detection or public visibility; this needs a mechanism V1 does not have
   390	(for example a witnessed public log), and is not supported by this amendment. (d) Rule other text. **(e) Adopt (a)
   391	and add protection against key substitution: the trust-file service-key listing of A10.4.1**, with its ordered
   392	classifier and authorization closure, scoped to relying parties using an authentic, trustworthy release. This is a
   393	BROADEN; it adds mechanism (rows 25–33). Rows 15–16 take effect on (a) or (e); rows 25–33 only on (e).
   394	
   395	**Owner recommendation: (e)**, which the Codex review of the decision memo supports. If the author picks (e) and the
   396	listing is later cut for schedule reasons while A9.5's independent release boundary is established, (a) applies
   397	and the absence of new-key protection is stated (row 33). Release-key custody (A10.4.2) is a separate ruling and
