     1	# Evidence for the A-UTC tolerance (Δ = 7200 s, m = 6) (2026-10-17)
     2	
     3	> **Status: RECOMMENDED (owner; the Codex review of the decision memo concurs), AWAITING AUTHOR RULING.** Drafted
     4	> 2026-10-17 by Claude (Opus 5.5) for Amendment 10 §A10.1.1, which cites it. It justifies the tolerance of assumption
     5	> A-UTC against the intended adversary. It does not establish that miners satisfy A-UTC: no data can. Adopting
     6	> A-UTC is a trust assumption that the author's signature would accept; the verifier cannot check it.
     7	>
     8	> Labels: **[F]** fact, with source; **[A]** assumption; **[E]** estimate or interpretation; **[R]** recommendation.
     9	> Mechanism details are from `docs/decisions/2026-10-17-utc-bound-and-capture-visibility.md` (the memo). Revised
    10	> 2026-10-17 after the Codex review `docs/reviews/amendment-10-r2/CODEX-REVIEW.md` (F01, F02, F03, F05, F07 apply
    11	> here; Codex's exact text); disposition in the same directory.
    12	
    13	## 1. What A-UTC says and what would falsify it
    14	
    15	**Notation (memo).** h* is the selected inclusion height of the receipt proof (confirmation one); h*+5 is
    16	confirmation six. r(j) is the real (UTC) time at which block j was first produced. nTime(j) is its header time.
    17	
    18	**[A] A-UTC (assumption_id `A-UTC-7200-6`).** At least one of the six blocks h* … h*+5 has
    19	`nTime(j) ≥ r(j) − 7200 s`: its header time is no more than 7200 s before it was actually mined.
    20	
    21	**Consequence (memo, Part 1; checked by Codex).** Under A-UTC and the registered commitment and history assumptions,
    22	the bytes and both signatures existed before `U = max nTime(h* … h*+5) + 7200 s`.
    23	
    24	**Failure condition.** A-UTC fails for a receipt exactly when **the miners of all six confirming blocks set lagging
    25	timestamps**: every block h* … h*+5 has a header time more than 7200 s before it was actually mined. Only then can
    26	U be earlier than the true existence time. (The weaker condition `max nTime + 7200 ≥ r(h*)` would itself suffice;
    27	A-UTC is a sufficient one-sided assumption, not a minimal one.)
    28	
    29	## 2. The intended adversary
    30	
    31	A9's adversary includes "anyone who … backdates receipts" (A9:40). Against U, a backdater has bytes that exist only
    32	at real time e, and wants a receipt whose U is earlier than e. The bytes must be committed in block h*, so
    33	e < r(h*); a backdater gains only if all six header times lag. Three cases:
    34	
    35	1. **A non-mining backdater** (a holder, a third party, or a captor of the service or its authority channels). It
    36	   cannot set any header time. It can only choose when to stamp, and which presented proof to use. It succeeds only
    37	   if a window of six consecutive blocks occurs in which every miner's timestamp lags by more than 7200 s. Section 3
    38	   reports the recorded margins in historical windows, with the limits of that record.
    39	2. **A backdater who bribes or controls miners.** To force failure independently of the other miners' behaviour, a
    40	   backdater would need to make all six timestamps lag production by more than 7200 s. Failure can also occur
    41	   without such control; it is the timestamp condition, not an attribution of miner intent. Consensus limits how far
    42	   each timestamp may lag (section 4). If a coalition finds an independent share α of blocks, a fixed
    43	   set of six is all theirs with probability α⁶: 7×10⁻⁴ at α = 0.3, 1.6% at α = 0.5 **[E]**. This is not a security
    44	   probability against an adversary who chooses when to submit, retries, withholds blocks or selects among proofs.
    45	   A coalition with a majority of hash power can keep timestamps behind real time without limit (A2.1).
    46	3. **Selection of a favourable proof.** The verifier selects the lowest qualifying height in the presented proof.
    47	   A presenter may still choose among available valid presentations, and later inclusion heights need not give
    48	   larger U values. Under A-UTC for the selected six-block segment, every resulting U remains a valid conditional
    49	   bound. Lowest-height selection supplies determinism for the presentation; it supplies no additional protection
    50	   against selecting a segment where A-UTC fails.
    51	
    52	**Why m = 6.** The six headers h* … h*+5 are already required, pinned and qualified for k = 6 (A2.1, profile §7.1).
    53	U needs no new input. A larger m would need more headers; a smaller m would rest the claim on fewer miners. m = 6 is
    54	not shown to be optimal **[F, Codex review]**.
    55	
    56	**Why Δ = 7200 s.** It is an author-selected tolerance. It equals Bitcoin's node-local forward tolerance
    57	(`MAX_FUTURE_BLOCK_TIME = 2*60*60`); that match is a convenience and does not validate it **[F, Codex review]**. The
    58	data in section 3 show the observed margin under it.
    59	
    60	## 3. Empirical header-time lag
    61	
    62	### 3.1 Primary source: the Bitcoin Block Arrival Time Dataset
    63	
    64	**[F] Source.** `bitcoin-data/block-arrival-times`, CC0 1.0, commit `1bb1c1b750dcc4633a54f01cbaaed8d789c3f4c0`
    65	(2026-03-28), https://github.com/bitcoin-data/block-arrival-times . Each row gives height, header hash and the
    66	millisecond time at which one observer node first received or connected the block. Observers include the KIT DSN
    67	Bitcoin monitoring nodes (https://www.dsn.kastel.kit.edu/bitcoin/), 0xB10C's peer-observer and monitoring nodes, and
    68	four independently operated Bitcoin Core nodes. Header times are from the dataset's
    69	`qa/block-timestamps/block-timestamps.csv` (SHA-256
    70	`85800b9110c2784f6711ebd348164f7de0a140692622251a4d8ee964a445046f`). All nineteen `data/*.csv` files, concatenated in
    71	C-locale filename order, hash to `335249f1099fdd2a6cb1d7d82766912488bdf6d81588d1f12023c369bb6c0b4c`
    72	(`LC_ALL=C bash -c 'cat data/*.csv' | sha256sum`, run in the dataset root). This all-files digest includes the two
    73	`stratum_work_*` CSVs; the statistical calculation excludes them. *(Dated correction 2026-10-17, review F07: the
    74	digest first given, `d83509d9…`, was not reproduced by the stated command, whose glob order depends on locale.)*
    75	
    76	**Method.** For each height, *first-seen* is the earliest arrival across all observers. The `stratum_work_*` files
    77	are excluded, because their row for height N records the arrival of block N−1. The lag of block j is
    78	`first-seen(j) − nTime(j)`. A block is first seen after it is produced, so this lag is **an upper bound on
    79	r(j) − nTime(j)**, the quantity A-UTC bounds, provided the observer clock is accurate. Computed 2026-10-17 by the
    80	script in section 6.
    81	
    82	**[F] Coverage.** 559,831 heights from 369,715 (2015-08-13) to 941,944 (2026-03-23), 97.8% of that range.
    83	
    84	**[F] Lag of single blocks** (first-seen minus nTime, seconds):
    85	
    86	| Statistic | Value |
    87	|---|---|
    88	| median | 18.1 |
    89	| 25th / 75th percentile | 9.0 / 29.0 |
    90	| 95th / 99th / 99.9th percentile | 48.4 / 80.0 / 237.0 |
    91	| maximum | 3,917.6 (height 409,854, 2016) |
    92	| blocks above 600 s / 1,800 s / 3,600 s / 7,200 s | 93 / 20 / 5 / 0 |
    93	| blocks below 0 s (header time after first-seen) | 34,461 (6.2%); minimum −4,417.8 |
    94	
    95	**[F] Six-block windows** (559,718 windows h … h+5 with all six heights covered):
    96	
    97	| Statistic | Value |
    98	|---|---|
    99	| largest "smallest lag in the window" | 1,064.9 s |
   100	| 99.9th percentile of "smallest lag in the window" | 30.1 s |
   101	| U minus first-seen(h) (U computed as max nTime(h … h+5) + 7200) | minimum 6,135.1 s; median 9,908.3 s |
   102	| X − 7200 = max nTime(h … h+5) − nTime(h) | median 2,727 s; 99th percentile 6,899 s |
   103	
   104	**[E] Interpretation.** In every retained complete six-block window, at least one recorded arrival lag was at most
   105	1064.9 s. The minimum computed U minus the inclusion block's recorded first-seen time was 6135.1 s; U's UTC
   106	interpretation remains conditional on A-UTC. If the selected observer readings do not understate production time,
   107	these observations support A-UTC for the sampled windows. Observer clock error is not bounded here. The dataset's
   108	±7200 s quality filter and excluded incomplete windows prevent an inference about the frequency of A-UTC failures
   109	outside the retained sample.
   110	
   111	**[F] Limits of this source.**
   112	- **Censoring at the threshold.** The dataset's CI (`qa/block-timestamps/check-block-timestamps.py`) fails if any
   113	  arrival differs from its header time by more than 7200 s ("The listed timestamps might have been recorded during
   114	  IBD or the system clock was wrong?"). The dataset therefore cannot contain a lag above 7200 s, whatever happened.
   115	  Its zero count above 7200 s is not evidence. Among the retained observations, none lies between 3918 s and
   116	  7200 s. This describes the selected sample; it does not remove the effect of the quality filter on inference about
   117	  miner behaviour.
   118	- **Observer clocks.** Lag includes propagation, observer delay and observer clock error. A fast observer clock
   119	  inflates the lag (conservative); a slow one understates it. The negative lags (6.2% of blocks) show miner clock
   120	  error, future-dated headers or observer clock error. The dataset does not separate them.
   121	- **Missing heights.** Windows with a missing height (2.2% of heights) are excluded. If rows were removed for
   122	  exceeding the CI threshold, the removal would hide exactly the windows of interest.
   123	- **History, not adversary.** The sample shows ordinary miner behaviour from 2015 to 2026. It does not bound a
   124	  coalition that sets lagging timestamps on purpose.
   125	
   126	### 3.2 Corroborating sources
   127	
   128	- **[F] mempool.space Block Time Report** (https://research.mempool.space/p/09defe3d-c836-44e7-b244-7f8434e72151).
   129	  One observer, arrival over the mempool.space websocket versus header time, 481 blocks between heights 857,514
   130	  and 858,080 (2024-08-19 to 2024-08-23): minimum −69 s, maximum +82 s, median 41 s, mean 40 s. The author describes
   131	  it as "quick and dirty" and attributes the negative values to drifted clocks at some pools (Braiins, F2Pool,
   132	  Foundry USA, Luxor, MARA Pool). Observer arrival, not production time.
   133	- **[F] Bowden, Keeler, Krzesinski and Taylor, "Block arrivals in the Bitcoin blockchain", arXiv:1801.07447
   134	  (2018), §II-B.** "Of the 500,000 blocks mined up to November 2017, 13,618 of them have a timestamp prior to that
   135	  of the previous block and some 1,000 of them have timestamps more than 10 minutes prior to the previous block
   136	  timestamp"; "Some inter-arrival times are more negative than −7,000 seconds but most are between −1,500 and 0
   137	  seconds." The authors attribute out-of-order times largely to future-dated headers followed by correct ones, and
   138	  to miners using the timestamp as an extra nonce. Our count from the dataset's header list over the same range is
   139	  13,638; the source of the 20-block difference is not established.
   140	- **[F] Chain-wide header order** (dataset header list, heights 1–942,357). 15,410 headers have nTime earlier than
   141	  their predecessor's. The largest backward step is 7,125 s. Since height 700,000 (2021-09): 1,040 headers, largest
   142	  backward step 2,032 s. A backward step measures disagreement between header times, not lag behind real time.
   143	
   144	## 4. What consensus forces
   145	
   146	**[F]** A header is invalid unless `nTime > MTP`, the median of the previous 11 header times (Bitcoin Core
   147	`validation.cpp`, "time-too-old"). So the largest lag a miner of block j can choose is less than r(j) − MTP(j). If
   148	r(j) − MTP(j) ≤ 7200 s, block j satisfies A-UTC whatever its miner does.
   149	
   150	**[F] Measured** with first-seen as an upper bound on r(j), over the same 559,831 blocks: first-seen(j) − MTP(j)
   151	has median 3,328 s, 99th percentile 7,824 s and maximum 21,779 s; it exceeds 7200 s for 1.9% of blocks. In only
   152	**545 of 559,718** six-block windows (0.097%) did it exceed 7200 s at all six blocks.
   153	
   154	**[E] Interpretation.** At the observed header history, 559173 of the 559718 complete windows contain a block with
   155	first-seen minus MTP at most 7200 s. Holding that block's actual predecessors fixed, and assuming its recorded
   156	arrival does not precede production, the MTP rule forces that block's timestamp within the tolerance. This is a
   157	diagnostic of the observed history. It does not show that the six miners could not violate A-UTC by changing their
   158	timestamps: earlier changes within the window can lower later MTPs. No count of windows requiring control outside
   159	the six is established here.
   160	
   161	## 5. Conclusion and what remains assumed
   162	
   163	- **[R]** Δ = 7200 s and m = 6 remain a proposed trust tolerance. The retained observations show substantial
   164	  recorded margins during ordinary operation, conditional on the observer-clock limitation. They do not validate the
   165	  tolerance against an intentional timestamp coalition or establish a failure probability. Author acceptance of
   166	  A-UTC remains necessary for the conditional UTC claim.
   167	- **[A]** The author's signature on A10, if it adopts §A10.1.1, adopts A-UTC as a trust assumption of the UTC
   168	  assessment. It does not establish that miners satisfy it. The verifier cannot check it; the bundle carries no
   169	  evidence of production times.
   170	- **Not decided here, recorded only:** whether a court or another examiner will accept A-UTC; whether a particular
   171	  use needs qualified (eIDAS) timestamps. RFC 3161 tokens over `SHA256(A_sig)` are a recorded candidate, not an
   172	  obligation (memo, U2).
   173	
   174	## 6. Reproduction
   175	
   176	Clone the dataset at the commit above into a fresh directory, keep the script elsewhere, and run
   177	`python3 -I lag.py <repo>`. To check the input digest, run `LC_ALL=C bash -c 'cat data/*.csv' | sha256sum` in the
   178	dataset root (C-locale filename order; all nineteen files, stratum files included). The script:
   179	
   180	```python
   181	import csv, sys, os, glob, statistics
   182	repo = sys.argv[1]
   183	nt = {int(h): int(t) for h, t in csv.reader(open(os.path.join(repo, 'qa/block-timestamps/block-timestamps.csv')))}
   184	first = {}                                   # height -> earliest arrival (ms) across observers
   185	for p in glob.glob(os.path.join(repo, 'data/*.csv')):
   186	    if os.path.basename(p).startswith('stratum'): continue
   187	    for r in csv.reader(open(p)):
   188	        h, ms = int(r[0]), int(r[2])
   189	        if h in nt and (h not in first or ms < first[h]): first[h] = ms
   190	lag = {h: first[h] / 1000 - nt[h] for h in first}
   191	mtp = lambda h: sorted(nt[h - i] for i in range(1, 12))[5]
   192	room = {h: first[h] / 1000 - mtp(h) for h in first}
   193	wins = [h for h in lag if all(h + i in lag for i in range(6))]
   194	win_min = [min(lag[h + i] for i in range(6)) for h in wins]
   195	margin = [max(nt[h + i] for i in range(6)) + 7200 - first[h] / 1000 for h in wins]
   196	all_room = sum(1 for h in wins if all(room[h + i] > 7200 for i in range(6)))
   197	print(len(lag), statistics.median(lag.values()), max(lag.values()), max(win_min), min(margin), all_room, len(wins))
   198	```

