   135	  of the previous block and some 1,000 of them have timestamps more than 10 minutes prior to the previous block
   136	  timestamp"; "Some inter-arrival times are more negative than −7,000 seconds but most are between −1,500 and 0
   137	  seconds." The authors attribute out-of-order times largely to future-dated headers followed by correct ones, and
   138	  to miners using the timestamp as an extra nonce. Our count from the dataset's header list over the same range is
   139	  13,638; the source of the 20-block difference is not established.
   140	- **[F] Chain-wide header order** (dataset header list, heights 1–942,357). 15,410 headers have nTime earlier than
   141	  their predecessor's. The largest backward step is 7,125 s. Since height 700,000 (2021-09): 1,040 headers, largest
   142	  backward step 2,032 s. A backward step measures disagreement between header times, not lag behind real time.
   143	
   144	## 4. What consensus forces
   145	
   146	**[F]** A header is invalid unless `nTime > MTP`, the median of the previous 11 header times (Bitcoin Core
   147	`validation.cpp`, "time-too-old"). So the largest lag a miner of block j can choose is less than r(j) − MTP(j). If
   148	r(j) − MTP(j) ≤ 7200 s, block j satisfies A-UTC whatever its miner does.
   149	
   150	**[F] Measured** with first-seen as an upper bound on r(j), over the same 559,831 blocks: first-seen(j) − MTP(j)
   151	has median 3,328 s, 99th percentile 7,824 s and maximum 21,779 s; it exceeds 7200 s for 1.9% of blocks. In only
   152	**545 of 559,718** six-block windows (0.097%) did it exceed 7200 s at all six blocks.
   153	
   154	**[E] Interpretation.** At the observed header history, 559173 of the 559718 complete windows contain a block with
   155	first-seen minus MTP at most 7200 s. Holding that block's actual predecessors fixed, and assuming its recorded
   156	arrival does not precede production, the MTP rule forces that block's timestamp within the tolerance. This is a
   157	diagnostic of the observed history. It does not show that the six miners could not violate A-UTC by changing their
   158	timestamps: earlier changes within the window can lower later MTPs. No count of windows requiring control outside
   159	the six is established here.
   160	
   161	## 5. Conclusion and what remains assumed
   162	
   163	- **[R]** Δ = 7200 s and m = 6 remain a proposed trust tolerance. The retained observations show substantial
   164	  recorded margins during ordinary operation, conditional on the observer-clock limitation. They do not validate the
   165	  tolerance against an intentional timestamp coalition or establish a failure probability. Author acceptance of
   166	  A-UTC remains necessary for the conditional UTC claim.
   167	- **[A]** The author's signature on A10, if it adopts §A10.1.1, adopts A-UTC as a trust assumption of the UTC
   168	  assessment. It does not establish that miners satisfy it. The verifier cannot check it; the bundle carries no
   169	  evidence of production times.
   170	- **Not decided here, recorded only:** whether a court or another examiner will accept A-UTC; whether a particular
   171	  use needs qualified (eIDAS) timestamps. RFC 3161 tokens over `SHA256(A_sig)` are a recorded candidate, not an
   172	  obligation (memo, U2).
