    16	## 1. Hardware
    17	
    18	- **If token custody is selected: YubiKey 1, the gpg signing token.** Holds the `gpg` slot's P-256 key, generated on the token, non-exportable.
    19	  PIN required; touch **off** for signing (the queue-fed signer runs unattended). It stops key export, not misuse:
    20	  whoever controls the queue has signing capability (A9 C3).
    21	- **YubiKey 2: the offline repository root.** Touch and PIN required. Generated on an **air-gapped machine**, with an
    22	  **encrypted offline backup** held separately. The generation and signing ceremony is written down and rehearsed
    23	  before first use. Its acts are limited to key publication and rotation commits and the end-of-issuance statement
    24	  (§4).
    25	- **Loss of the selected slot-2 token.** Issuance stops. Loss cannot be told apart from theft, so the current window is treated as
    26	  compromised until its not-after: a published window cannot be shortened (A9.6). Issuance resumes only under a new
    27	  key, which needs a registered rotation mechanism (A9.6), or not at all.
    28	- **Loss of YubiKey 2.** Restore the root from the encrypted offline backup, by the written ceremony. If the backup
    29	  is also lost, no new key assertion can be signed by the key in the verifiers' trust configuration: that needs a
    30	  new verifier release, and every relying party must update. Existing receipts are unaffected.
    31	
    32	- **If slot-2 custody is a token (A9.6, chosen before 30 Oct), YubiKey 1 is in every receipt's signing path.** Issuance
    33	  throughput and availability are those of one USB token on the author's host: if that host, its power or its
    34	  network is down, issuance stops. This follows from prereg §3 C (a local signer in a failure domain independent of
    35	  AWS) made physical by the author's hardware requirement. It would be acceptable for V1 (one domain, about a dozen
    36	  test receipts). It contradicts the product thesis ("cheap, easy") at any real volume.
    37	  - *Measure before the 30 Oct freeze:* sustained signatures per second on the real token, using a throwaway key
    38	    and resetting the applet afterwards. Until measured, any throughput figure is a prediction.
    39	  - **Alternative V1 custody (A9.6):** an HSM-generated non-exportable P-256 key at a non-AWS provider, used through the external queue-pulling policy signer. The dated profile choice records the provider, key version, policy boundary, OpenPGP adapter and immutable public-key packet. Its authority is evidenced through the same DNSSEC and signed, anchored git key-publication record.
    40	  - **Deadline (A9.6) [OWNER 2026-10-14, r2-F17].** For the hybrid, the final custody implementation must have
    41	    completed the deployed-slot pass-condition evidence by **21 Oct**. Selection may be deferred to before 30 Oct only
    42	    among custody implementations that each met that deadline; otherwise the registered fallback is taken
    43	    (V1-PROFILE §14.2 item 5).
    44	
    45	- **Signer isolation (OWNER DECISION 2026-10-13, prompted by the author's three-cloud proposal).** Each signer runs
    46	  inside its own provider's private network with no inbound path. It pulls requests from a queue and pushes
    47	  signatures back. Each key accepts use only from inside that network: in AWS, a KMS key-policy condition on the VPC
    48	  endpoint; in GCP, a VPC Service Controls perimeter around Cloud KMS. Stolen credentials alone then cannot sign.
    49	  There is no cross-cloud private network, because it would be a shared component coupling the two failure domains.
    50	  *V2 note (not registered):* the public API moves to a third provider (Azure), so capture of one signer's cloud
    51	  yields one slot, not two. Capture of the API layer still yields both, until there is separation of control.
    52	- **Monoculture check (open, before 30 Oct).** Heterogeneous layers are defeated when the components underneath turn
    53	  out to be identical (the author's procurement observation). Before choosing slot-2 custody, record which HSM
    54	  vendor and certification sits under AWS KMS and under GCP Cloud HSM. If both slots rest on the same HSM product,
    55	  their failure domains are less independent than the design assumes. *Not yet checked; treat any statement about
    56	  it as a prediction.*
    57	
    58	## 2. Operating numbers
    59	
    60	- **Window length:** fixed when the key-publication record is published; at most 90 days (A9 invariant,
    61	  `DNS_WINDOW_LENGTH`).
    62	- **Retirement margin: 96 h = δ + 24 h.** The issuer stops signing 96 h before `issuance-not-after`. This is an
    63	  owner-selected operating reserve, not a proved signing-to-anchor bound: δ is a declared-time-to-chain-time
    64	  tolerance, and A2.1 gives no finite backward-skew bound (Codex finding 2). Safety does not depend on it: an attempt
    65	  whose T would exceed not-after is not shipped (A9.6), whatever the margin.
    66	- **Rotation plan.** Before the stop point of the single 90-day window, record one of: a rotation mechanism
    67	  registered by amendment (multiple records or per-key owner names), or the end of issuance (§4).
    68	
    69	## 3. Slot-2 declared-time check
    70	
    71	The `gpg`-slot signer compares each frame's `declared_issue_time` with its own clock and refuses to sign if the
    72	difference exceeds ε (24 h, A9.2). This limits an AWS-side takeover to minting at the current time (A9.6); it
    73	does not stop it. A clock fault on the signer host shows up as refusals, which fail safe. For HSM custody, the external policy signer enforces this check before submitting the OpenPGP digest; AWS has no direct key-use permission.
    74	
    75	## 4. Exit procedure
    76	
    77	When V1 stops issuing:
    78	1. **Disable issuance:** stop the API and the queue consumer; the issuer signs nothing more.
    79	2. **Initiate key destruction:** schedule KMS key deletion; initiate destruction of the selected slot-2 key (token applet reset, or the non-AWS provider's key-version destruction procedure). Scheduling a KMS
    80	   deletion starts a waiting period; it is not destruction.
    81	3. **Record completion** of destruction as an operational fact, with the date, once the KMS deletion has completed
    82	   and the selected slot-2 key's destruction has completed. If a token is lost, record that instead (§1).
    83	4. **Publish an end-of-issuance statement** as a git commit signed by the repository root (YubiKey 2), OTS-stamped on
    84	   its signed commit id.
    85	
    86	Verification of existing receipts needs no private key. No V1 verifier rule consults the statement; it is evidence
    87	for a later examiner. After the exit, the window's not-after, not the statement, is what bounds minting (A9.6
    88	guarantee 2, in chain time).
    89	
    90	## 5. Issuance gate: chain-order pre-commitment (A9 C4; OWNER 2026-10-14, G02)
    91	
    92	**Issuer duty.** Do not issue in a window until the key-assertion commit's stamp has k = 6 confirmations and the
    93	receipt will anchor at a higher height (chain-order pre-commitment, A9 C4).
    94	
    95	- Before the first receipt of a window: obtain the key-assertion commit's upgraded OTS proof, validate it offline
    96	  under the V1 rules (qualifying attestation at height h_git, headers h_git … h_git + 5 present and pinned), and
    97	  record h_git and T_git.
    98	- The verifier requires `h_git + 5 < h_receipt` (`INVALID / GIT_NOT_PRECOMMITTED`). A receipt whose stamp is
    99	  submitted after the issuer has observed block h_git + 5 is mined above it, so the wait satisfies the rule. No
   100	  clock comparison with `declared_issue_time` is made (owner, 2026-10-14).
   101	- The issuer still checks both rules on each receipt's own evidence before shipping (a receipt ships only when
   102	  complete, A9.1). A receipt that fails either is not shipped; it is re-stamped or abandoned.
   103	- **Pre-ship checks for the profile's fail-closed completions** [OWNER 2026-10-14, r2-F07, r2-F08, r2-F09,
   104	  r2-F10]. Before shipping, also check on the receipt's own evidence: the selected path of each of the three proofs
   105	  (receipt, snapshot, git) has the transaction-shape subset and the conventional merkle suffix (V1-PROFILE §15.2;
   106	  the git proof is checked once per window, when h_git is recorded); the snapshot body has no RRSIG with fewer
   107	  Labels than its owner (§15.3); every OpenPGP signature (both slots, possession, the repository commit) carries
   108	  type-16/type-33 subpackets only in the shapes V1-PROFILE §8 allows. A failure means re-stamp, re-capture or
   109	  re-sign before shipping, never ship. All 352 Bitcoin paths in the repository's 129 proofs pass the proof checks
   110	  (owner check 2026-10-14).
   111	- This is chain order, not UTC (A2.1). It does not establish that the assertion was published before issue.
   112	
   113	## 6. Release key (A9.5 trust-configuration authority; OWNER 2026-10-14, G08)
   114	
   115	**Custody: OPEN.** Not chosen. Due before the 30 Oct freeze.
   116	
   117	- **What it does.** Signs each verifier release. Each release compiles in the SHA-256 of exactly one trust
   118	  configuration and the frozen spec catalog; the verifier refuses any other configuration
   119	  (`TRUST_CONFIG_UNAUTHENTICATED`, no verdict). The trust configuration itself carries no signature.
   120	- **Independence requirement.** The release key must be unusable by the DNS credential, the repository root
   121	  (YubiKey 2), the issuing AWS account, and any service those can invoke. Key inequality is not enough: the dated
   122	  custody record must name the key, its holder and its access boundary, and show that capture of any
   123	  authority-channel credential grants no ability to sign a release, authorize a configuration hash, or replace the
   124	  compiled trust root. CI is not the authenticity check.
   125	- **Not YubiKey 2.** The repository root signs key assertions; if it also authorized releases, capture of one
   126	  channel credential would let an attacker distribute a configuration trusting their own keys and pins, collapsing
   127	  n = 2.
   128	- **Candidates, not decided:** a third hardware token, generated air-gapped with an encrypted offline backup and a
   129	  written ceremony like YubiKey 2's; or an HSM key at a provider that holds none of the channel credentials. YubiKey 2
   130	  itself is not yet in hand.
   131	- **Before each release:** run the release checker (G09 invariants: immutable entries and pins, nondecreasing
   132	  `as_of`, version + 1, `previous_sha256` chain, non-retroactive closing of open ends), then sign. Close a KSK's open
   133	  end before `as_of` passes IANA's announced retirement.
   134	- **Bootstrap and rotation.** Publish the release public key with the verifier documentation and relying-party
   135	  text, and record its fingerprint in the freeze record. A new release key is introduced only by a release signed
   136	  with the current one. The procedure is OPEN with custody.
   137	- **Loss or compromise.** Compromise is distribution compromise, a stated A9 boundary: a malicious release could
   138	  trust attacker keys and pins and so accept forged evidence. Existing receipts stay verifiable under genuine
   139	  releases, and relying parties must obtain a release under a new key by some authenticated path. Loss stops new
   140	  releases (configuration updates, new pins) until a recovery path exists. Both procedures are OPEN with custody.
   141	- **Required test before freeze:** a captured repository signer, a captured DNS credential and the issuing AWS
   142	  account each try to authorize a configuration; the verifier refuses all three.
   143	- **Tracked as freeze items** [OWNER 2026-10-14, r2-F17]. Bootstrap, rotation and bounded loss/compromise recovery
   144	  for the release key, and the exact signed release preimage and verification format, are named items of
   145	  V1-PROFILE §14.2 (items 1 and 2). They stay operations; none becomes a bundle-verifier rule.
# Non-author review: Amendment 10, revision 2

2026-10-17. **Result: revise before signing.** Eight findings: five MEDIUM and three LOW. No HIGH or CRITICAL defect found. The conditional UTC inequality is sound under A-UTC and the existing commitment/history assumptions. The ordered service-key classifier and authorization closure support the stated non-acceptance guarantee. The material corrections concern what the tolerance evidence establishes, counterfactual mining and proof selection, the plain custody explanation, and change accounting. This review makes no author ruling.

MEDIUM means a material correction to the proposed claim, its supporting argument, or its change record. LOW means a bounded precision or reproducibility correction that does not defeat either construction.

## Reviewed artifacts and validation

Read A10 in full, the tolerance evidence file including its embedded reproduction script, the complete decision memo, A9 in full at signing commit `2ad34890359c446efaebcb116388b8e9ffbf6428`, V1-PROFILE §5/§7/§14.2/§15.5 and its output provisions, and STE-STYLE in full. Also checked the memo's prior review and disposition to distinguish its recommendations from new evidence. Repository HEAD: `7d7f94d797048f96df9a2b129962a5604439f4ae`. A10 and the decision documents were untracked proposed material.

All A10 locators below refer to `docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-10.md`; “evidence” means `docs/decisions/2026-10-17-a-utc-tolerance-evidence.md`. The reviewed A10 SHA-256 is `55f8a57ce0b5bafba50633069259605b5d29091748849ef9b24883f709367c8f`; the evidence SHA-256 is `71abfa3e5213bf4fc24aa592106b2bbef09f0731088717a06cad0f31c53be8d3`.

Cloned the primary dataset outside the workspace and verified HEAD equals the cited commit `1bb1c1b750dcc4633a54f01cbaaed8d789c3f4c0`. Extracted the evidence's fenced Python script verbatim and ran `python3 -I lag.py <dataset-repo>`. Its output was:

```text
559831 18.124000072479248 3917.6289999485016 1064.8840000629425 6135.137000083923 545 559718
```

These reproduce the reported block/window counts, median and maximum lag, largest window-minimum lag, minimum reported margin, and the 545-window MTP statistic. An additional calculation reproduced the median margin, `9908.319000005722` seconds, and the single-block threshold counts `93 / 20 / 5 / 0`. These are statistics of the retained observations, subject to F01 and F02. The header-timestamp CSV digest matches. The concatenated-data digest does not: F07.

Read the dataset's actual quality-check code and source descriptions. Its README confirms the two-hour QA restriction; the source descriptions confirm why the stratum files must be excluded. [Pinned dataset](https://github.com/bitcoin-data/block-arrival-times/tree/1bb1c1b750dcc4633a54f01cbaaed8d789c3f4c0), [QA implementation](https://github.com/bitcoin-data/block-arrival-times/blob/1bb1c1b750dcc4633a54f01cbaaed8d789c3f4c0/qa/block-timestamps/check-block-timestamps.py).

The mempool.space page was fetched directly and its 481-block count and −69/+82/41/40-second statistics match. Bowden et al. §II-B contains the cited header-order observations. Neither source supplies a production-clock bound. [mempool.space report](https://research.mempool.space/p/09defe3d-c836-44e7-b244-7f8434e72151), [Bowden et al.](https://arxiv.org/html/1801.07447v1#S2.SS2).

## Findings and exact fixes

### F01 — MEDIUM: the empirical conclusions drop the censoring and clock qualifications

**Locations:** A10 :73–74, :257–271; evidence :95–110 and :149–152.

The measurements reproduce. The limitations are candidly recorded in the technical body, but the interpretation says A-UTC held and the plain summary says measurements found no six-block failure. Those are stronger statements than this dataset establishes. The dataset rejects arrivals outside ±7200 seconds of header time, and no lower bound on observer clock error is supplied. Taking the earliest observer arrival can select a slow clock. Propagation delay alone makes arrival lag conservative; an unbounded negative clock error does not.

Writing an observer's recorded arrival as `a = r + propagation_delay + clock_error`, the inference `r − nTime ≤ a − nTime` needs `propagation_delay + clock_error ≥ 0`. Evidence :70 acknowledges that dependency. The interpretation and conclusion must retain it. The absence of retained observations between 3918 and 7200 seconds is descriptive evidence about this selected sample; it does not validate the excluded tail, the uncensored frequency of A-UTC failure, or an adversarial success probability. The sample also excludes windows with missing heights.

**Exact fix:** replace evidence :95–98 with:

> In every retained complete six-block window, at least one recorded arrival lag was at most 1064.9 s. The minimum computed U minus the inclusion block's recorded first-seen time was 6135.1 s; U's UTC interpretation remains conditional on A-UTC. If the selected observer readings do not understate production time, these observations support A-UTC for the sampled windows. Observer clock error is not bounded here. The dataset's ±7200 s quality filter and excluded incomplete windows prevent an inference about the frequency of A-UTC failures outside the retained sample.

Replace evidence :104–105's “distribution below the threshold is not censored” sentence with:

> Among the retained observations, none lies between 3918 s and 7200 s. This describes the selected sample; it does not remove the effect of the quality filter on inference about miner behaviour.

Replace the first conclusion bullet at evidence :149–152 with:

> [R] Δ = 7200 s and m = 6 remain a proposed trust tolerance. The retained observations show substantial recorded margins during ordinary operation, conditional on the observer-clock limitation. They do not validate the tolerance against an intentional timestamp coalition or establish a failure probability. Author acceptance of A-UTC remains necessary for the conditional UTC claim.

In the plain summary replace :73–74 with these bullets:

> - The retained measurements cover 2015 to 2026. In each complete set of six, one recorded arrival was no later than chain time plus 1065 seconds.
> - The dataset rejects arrival times more than two hours from the chain time. It cannot show failures beyond that limit.
> - Observer clocks can also be wrong. These measurements do not show that A-UTC was true.

Keep A10 :268–271's technical limitations. Do not replace them with an unconditional empirical assurance elsewhere.


